  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 233 PM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nThursday morning, a ridge looks to be overhead at 500-mb, with a \nsurface high to the east. At the same time, ensemble guidance is \nconsistent with a surface low across South-Central Canada and \nportions of the Northern United States. This would favor southerly \nsurface flow across the forecast region. Forecast temperatures are \ncurrently in the mid to upper 80s across the forecast region. NBM \nguidance does suggest a 20% chance or greater across a large portion \nof the forecast area that high temperatures will reach back into the \n90s, with a near 50% chance across localized portions of the Tri-\nState Area. However, in order for this to occur, winds and solar \nheating would need to increase. LREF guidance suggests that there is \naround a 50-60% chance that cloud cover will remain below 20% \ncoverage, with the NBM suggesting an 80-90% chance for the same. In \naddition, the surface high to the east and surface low to the north \nboth look to be in favorable positions relative to the upper level \nflow for intensification, which could enhance the southerly flow \nacross the forecast area. If these conditions can be met, hot \nconditions may redevelop across the area Thursday. \n\nBy Friday, upper level troughing looks to be pushing into the United \nStates from the northwest. Winds are forecast to be out of the north \nto northwest as the leading edge of a high protrudes into the \nCentral High Plains during the afternoon. Forecast highs are in the \nlow to mid 90s despite the favored northerly component to the \nsurface flow, as NBM guidance shows a 50% chance or higher that \nwinds will remain below 10 mph, particularly across Northwest \nKansas. Guidance is also in favor of low cloud cover, which would \nallow solar heating to override the effects of the northerly surface \nflow. The biggest point to note is that relative humidities are \nforecast to drop into the lower 20s and possibly high teens across \nportions of Eastern Colorado. This could indicate the possibility \nfor localized fire weather conditions, especially across Yuma County \nin Colorado where NBM guidance suggests up to a 2 in 3 chance that \nwinds will reach or exceed 25 mph (one of the thresholds for fire \nweather). This will be a trend to keep an eye on as we approach the \nupcoming weekend. \n\nTroughing is forecast to continue approaching the forecast area \ngoing into Saturday. Highs are forecast in the upper 80s and lower \n90s on Saturday ahead of the trough. Lee cyclogenesis is possible at \nthe leading edge of the trough as it crosses the Colorado Rockies, \nwhich could aid in the development of showers and thunderstorms \nSaturday evening and overnight. Severe thunderstorms seem unlikely, \nas LREF guidance suggests that storms will only have a few hundred \nJ/kg of surface CAPE (a measure of atmospheric instability) to take \nadvantage of. All areas have a 30% chance or less of receiving \ngreater than 0.1 inches of precipitation from these storms based on \n24 hr precipitation guidance from the NBM. \n\nTemperatures are expected to fall once the trough passes over the \nforecast area, though the timing is very uncertain at this time. The \ntrough may pass through as early as Sunday, which is reflected in \nthe forecast highs of the upper 70s and mid 80s. However, as\nlong as the trough remains west of the forecast area, similar\nconditions to Saturday could be expected. Showers and \nthunderstorms are forecast on Sunday as well, though chances \nare not much better than that of Saturday.",
